  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 255 AM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Seasonable to slightly above average \ntemperatures and dry conditions through Saturday.\n\nMoisture at the upper levels is streaming north and east from \nHurricane Isaias early this morning, leading to a high level cloud \ndeck spreading across the local region. Sfc high pressure is in \nplace over the Mid Atlantic and Great Lakes region today with a cold \nfront situated to the south. Today will be on the warm side despite \nthe clouds with highs in the mid to upper 70s. Cooler tomorrow as \nwinds become onshore and breezy. Highs will be in the low 70s. Lows \ntonight will be in 50s to around 60 for most and in the mid 60s \nclose to the Albemarle Sound. Will note that statistical models \nindicate temps as low as the upper 40s in the piedmont. Mostly dry \nthrough tomorrow evening, but a few showers could sneak into south-\ncentral VA as early as late Saturday afternoon. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain chances return Saturday night through early \nMonday. \n\nThe forecast for the latter half of the weekend has held fairly \nsteady since yesterday. There is increasing confidence that moisture \ngets pulled northward and then pushed eastward across the region as \nIsaias makes landfall and merges with an upper trough in vicinity of \nthe Mid-South. Confidence in timing has increased some as the model \nguidance has came into better agreement for the onset of accumulated \nrainfall, showing the 06-12z Sunday timeframe. The 00z ensembles \ncontinue to show PW values aob 200% of normal along with a plume of \nminor instability (<500 J/kg) surging N ahead of the trough. This \ncould lead to heavy rainfall for areas and the slight chance for \nthunderstorms on Sunday. The highest rainfall totals will likely be \nin the southern half of the area as there is a hint in the guidance \nof more enhanced rainfall along a coastal trough. The presence of a \ncoastal trough would also suggest a band of drier air setting up \nsomewhere over the area. This could lead to a large spread in \nrainfall totals across the area. There is still spread between the \n10th and 90th percentile QPF in the 00z/09 ensemble guidance, but \namounts generally trended up again. Several ensembles now show a \n10th percentile (reasonable low end) QPF of over an inch across the \nsouth and ~0.75\" elsewhere and a 90th percentile (reasonable high \nend) of a widespread 2-4\" with localized areas upwards of 5\". Leaned \nheavily on the WPC QPF, which is close to the mean amounts from the \nmodels. This yielded 2-3\" in the southeast, around 2\" in \nLouisa/Fluvanna, and around 1.5\" in between where that possible dry \nslot could set up.  \n\nThe coastal trough would also suggest an in situ \nwedge resulting in cooler temperatures and more stable conditions \nSunday across the Piedmont. Deeper moisture erodes Sunday night into \nMonday. However, there are lingering PoPs as the upper trough swings \nacross the region. Behind this system, drier air will filter back in \nlater Monday through the middle of the week. Temperatures should \nremain near average late this weekend through the middle of next \nweek.",
